  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved legal and regulatory framework for enterprise development</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Moldova, women account for only one-third of entrepreneurs, the vast majority, owning micro-companies. Gender constraints in the business environment create significant challenges for women-owned businesses. One of the key barriers is the disproportionate burden of childcare and household responsibilities, which limits women entrepreneurs to working an average of eight hours less per week than their male counterparts.  

Aligned with Moldova&#x2019;s new Programme on Women&#x2019;s Entrepreneurship and other relevant policies, the ILO provides targeted support to women entrepreneurs across various sectors. The interventions focus on connecting women business owners with available care services, assisting them in balancing work and family responsibilities, and ensuring access to other gender-responsive support services. The ILO supports the development of an assessment to evaluate the demand and supply of services, including care services, to better understand how they align with the needs of women entrepreneurs. 

In addition, the ILO works with selected women&#x2019;s entrepreneurship associations to strengthen their advocacy and communication capacities, particularly in relation to care services. This effort also involves linking these associations with the National Employers&#x2019; Organization of Moldova (CNPM) to enhance women&#x2019;s representation, amplify their voices, and expand CNPM&#x2019;s reach throughout the country. 

As a result of these interventions, constituents, women&#x2019;s entrepreneurship associations, and service providers will be better equipped to respond to the needs of women entrepreneurs. Awareness will increase regarding how programmes, initiatives, and support services can be designed to address the specific challenges faced by women business owners, particularly in relation to care services. Furthermore, women entrepreneurs will gain greater access to and make increased use of support services, enabling them to improve and grow their businesses more effectively.</narrative>
  </description>
